QB.push(
{i:91,d:1,t:"1.5",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"Your team debates whether a rule belongs in a hook or in the system prompt. The rule is: <em>redact customer card numbers from any tool result before the model sees them.</em>",
ask:"Which mechanism is correct and why?",
a:["A <code>PostToolUse</code> hook, because redaction must happen before the data reaches the model and must never depend on the model's cooperation",
"A system prompt instruction telling the model to ignore any card numbers it sees",
"A tool description warning that results may contain sensitive data",
"A post-processing step that redacts card numbers from the model's final output"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Redaction is a deterministic transformation that must occur upstream of the model, which is exactly what a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook does.",
"Once the data is in the context it has already been sent to the model and logged in the transcript. Instructing the model to ignore it does not undo that.",
"A description is metadata for tool selection and cannot transform a payload.",
"Too late by two steps: the data has already entered the context, and the model may have used it in reasoning that leaks into the output in other forms."],
e:"The decisive question for any data-handling rule is <em>where in the pipeline must this be true?</em> Redaction must be true before the model sees the data, which places it in a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook &mdash; intercepting the tool result on its way back. The same hook position handles format normalisation, unit conversion and trimming verbose payloads to relevant fields. Anything downstream of the model is too late, and anything relying on the model's cooperation is probabilistic where a guarantee is required.",
k:"<code>PostToolUse</code> sits between tool result and model. Redaction, normalisation and trimming all belong there &mdash; upstream of the model, in code."},

{i:92,d:1,t:"1.5",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"You need to block an outgoing tool call when its arguments violate policy, and you need to transform incoming tool results before the model reads them.",
ask:"Which hooks handle these two jobs?",
a:["<code>PreToolUse</code> for blocking the outgoing call, <code>PostToolUse</code> for transforming the incoming result",
"<code>PostToolUse</code> for both, since it has access to the full call and result",
"<code>PreToolUse</code> for both, since it runs first",
"Neither &mdash; blocking requires a permission rule and transformation requires a separate proxy service"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The names describe position relative to tool execution, and that position determines what each can do.",
"<code>PostToolUse</code> runs after execution, so it cannot prevent a call from happening &mdash; the refund has already been issued.",
"<code>PreToolUse</code> runs before execution, so the result does not exist yet and cannot be transformed.",
"Permission rules are a valid additional mechanism, but hooks handle both jobs and no external proxy is required."],
e:"Anchor on position. <code>PreToolUse</code> fires after the model has decided to call a tool but before your code executes it &mdash; the only place you can block, modify arguments, or inject system-determined values. <code>PostToolUse</code> fires after execution but before the result enters the model's context &mdash; the only place you can normalise, redact or trim. Exam questions about enforcement point at <code>PreToolUse</code>; questions about data shape or context bloat point at <code>PostToolUse</code>.",
k:"<code>PreToolUse</code>: block, modify arguments, inject values. <code>PostToolUse</code>: normalise, redact, trim results. Position determines capability."},

{i:93,d:1,t:"1.5",s:"CSA",diff:2,
q:"A <code>lookup_order</code> MCP tool returns 40 fields per order, of which the agent ever uses five. Across a long conversation this bloats the context substantially.",
ask:"What is the cleanest fix?",
a:["A <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that projects the response down to the five fields the agent actually needs",
"Ask the agent in its prompt to ignore the fields it does not need",
"Increase the context window so the extra fields fit comfortably",
"Summarise the conversation more aggressively once it grows long"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Trimming verbose tool output to relevant fields is the named remedy, and a hook applies it deterministically on every call.",
"The tokens are consumed the moment the result enters the context; the model cannot un-read them by being told to ignore them.",
"Buys headroom while continuing to waste it, and the same conversation on a busier account fills it again.",
"Summarisation is lossy and reactive &mdash; it degrades exactly the specific values you care about, to fix bloat you could have prevented."],
e:"The blueprint calls out this pattern explicitly: tool outputs accumulate in context disproportionately to their relevance, with 40 fields returned where 5 are needed. The remedy is a projection in a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook, applied once and consistently. Note the ordering of good practice &mdash; prevent bloat at the source (trim output, scope return payloads, delegate verbose work) before reaching for mitigation (compaction, summarisation, bigger windows).",
k:"Trim verbose tool results to needed fields in a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook. Prevention beats compaction; compaction is lossy about specifics."},

{i:94,d:1,t:"1.5",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"An engineer implements a hook that logs every tool call to an audit system, but the logging service is occasionally slow and the hook has no timeout. During an outage, all agent conversations hang.",
ask:"What is the design error?",
a:["A non-essential hook was placed synchronously in the critical path with no timeout or failure isolation",
"Hooks should never perform I/O of any kind",
"Audit logging should be done by the model, not by a hook",
"The hook should have been a <code>PostToolUse</code> rather than a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Distinguish hooks that must succeed for correctness from hooks that are observational, and isolate the latter.",
"Too absolute &mdash; enforcement hooks legitimately query state stores and permission services. The rule is about timeouts and criticality, not I/O.",
"Audit logging must not depend on the model's cooperation; that would make the audit trail as unreliable as the thing it audits.",
"The position is irrelevant to the failure. An unbounded synchronous call hangs from either position."],
e:"Classify each hook by whether the agent's correctness depends on it. Enforcement hooks (block a refund over the limit, inject a tenant id) must run synchronously and should fail closed &mdash; if the check cannot be performed, deny. Observational hooks (audit logs, metrics, traces) should have tight timeouts and fail open, or be dispatched asynchronously, so an outage in a telemetry system never takes down the agent. Every hook needs an explicit answer to &ldquo;what happens when this hook itself fails?&rdquo;",
k:"Enforcement hooks: synchronous, fail closed. Observational hooks: timeout-bounded or async, fail open. Always define hook-failure behaviour."},

{i:95,d:1,t:"1.5",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"Compliance requires that every refund is tagged with the acting agent's identity and the customer's tenant id. Today the model supplies both as tool arguments, and audits find occasional mismatches.",
ask:"Which change guarantees correctness?",
a:["Inject both values in a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook from authenticated session state, overriding anything the model supplied",
"Mark both fields as <code>required</code> in the tool's JSON Schema",
"Add the values to the system prompt so the model always has them available",
"Validate the values after the refund and raise an alert on mismatch"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. System-determined values are injected by code from authenticated state, never requested from the model.",
"<code>required</code> guarantees the fields are present, not that they are correct. The model can supply a wrong tenant id in a required field.",
"Making the values available improves the odds and does nothing to guarantee them, especially across long conversations where salience drifts.",
"Detects the problem after a refund has been misattributed &mdash; useful monitoring, not a control."],
e:"Two exam favourites intersect. First, schemas constrain <em>structure</em> while hooks control <em>content</em>: <code>required</code> and <code>enum</code> cannot make the model choose the right tenant. Second, anything the model asserts is untrusted input; identity and tenancy come from the authenticated session your code owns. Injecting them in <code>PreToolUse</code> also simplifies the tool schema, because they stop being model-facing parameters at all.",
k:"Never let the model supply identity, tenancy or audit fields. Inject them in <code>PreToolUse</code> from authenticated state."},

{i:96,d:1,t:"1.5",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"A hook is written to enforce a business rule, but it is registered only in one developer's local Agent SDK configuration. Production runs without it.",
ask:"What does this reveal about where enforcement should live?",
a:["Enforcement must be part of the deployed server-side configuration or the tool implementation, not a per-developer client setting",
"Hooks are inherently unsuitable for business rules and should be replaced by prompts",
"The rule should be duplicated into every developer's local configuration",
"Production should run without hooks to keep behaviour predictable"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A control that can be absent is not a control; it must live where it cannot be omitted.",
"Hooks are exactly the right mechanism &mdash; the failure is in deployment, not in the choice of mechanism.",
"Relies on every developer configuring it correctly forever, which is the same weakness with more copies.",
"Removes the enforcement entirely, which is the opposite of what compliance requires."],
e:"Ask of every control: can it be missing? A hook that lives in local client configuration can be absent, misconfigured or bypassed. The durable placements are the deployed agent service's own configuration (versioned, reviewed, promoted through environments) or, most robustly, inside the tool implementation itself &mdash; a server-side precondition on <code>process_refund</code> holds no matter which client calls it, including a client that is not your agent at all.",
k:"Controls must live where they cannot be omitted: deployed server-side config, or inside the tool. Client-side-only enforcement is bypassable."},

{i:97,d:1,t:"1.5",s:"DPT",diff:2,
q:"Two MCP servers return status as different types: one uses numeric codes (<code>2</code>), the other uses strings (<code>\"active\"</code>). The agent frequently compares them incorrectly and draws wrong conclusions.",
ask:"What is the best fix?",
a:["Normalise both to a single canonical representation in a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook before the results reach the model",
"Explain the two encodings in the system prompt and let the model translate",
"Ask the MCP server maintainers to standardise, and accept the errors until they do",
"Add few-shot examples showing correct comparisons between the two encodings"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Deterministic mapping between known encodings is a code problem with a code answer, applied once and centrally.",
"Puts a mechanical translation into the model's reasoning on every single comparison. It will mostly work and sometimes not.",
"Fixing upstream is genuinely worth pursuing, but it is slow, may be outside your control, and leaves you broken meanwhile.",
"Examples cannot make a translation deterministic, and the failure mode is silent."],
e:"Data normalisation is the textbook <code>PostToolUse</code> use case, listed in the blueprint alongside Unix timestamps, ISO 8601 dates and numeric status codes. The reasoning generalises: any transformation with a known, deterministic mapping should be done in code, freeing the model's attention for judgement. As a bonus, normalising at the boundary means new MCP servers with yet another encoding are handled by extending one mapping rather than by re-teaching the model.",
k:"Deterministic mapping &rarr; code. Normalise heterogeneous tool outputs at the boundary so the model sees one consistent representation."},

{i:98,d:1,t:"1.5",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"You want a hook that blocks a tool call, but only when a runtime condition holds that depends on data returned by an earlier tool in the same session.",
ask:"What does this require?",
a:["Session-scoped state that hooks can read and write, so the <code>PreToolUse</code> hook can evaluate the condition against what has already happened",
"Scanning the conversation history inside the hook to reconstruct what happened",
"Passing the condition as a parameter that the model supplies on the call",
"Restructuring so the condition is evaluated by the model rather than the hook"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Stateful enforcement needs a state store the hooks own, keyed by session, independent of the transcript.",
"Workable in simple cases but fragile: it infers state from text the model influences, and a tool <em>call</em> does not prove what the tool <em>returned</em>.",
"Model-supplied conditions are untrusted input, so the check would be enforcing whatever the model claimed.",
"Moves a guarantee back into probabilistic territory, which is the opposite of what a hook is for."],
e:"This is the same structure as the identity-verification precondition: the check needs authoritative state, and the transcript is not authoritative. Maintain a session store that hooks and tools both read and write &mdash; <code>get_customer</code> writes <code>verified_customer_id</code>, and the <code>PreToolUse</code> hook on <code>process_refund</code> reads it. Because your code owns the store, no amount of conversational manoeuvring can fabricate the precondition.",
k:"Stateful enforcement needs a session store your code owns. The transcript is model-influenced and is not an authoritative record of state."},

{i:99,d:1,t:"1.5",s:"CSA",diff:2,
q:"A hook blocks a call and returns the message: <code>{\"error\": \"blocked\"}</code>. The agent tells the customer &ldquo;there was a system error&rdquo; and the conversation stalls.",
ask:"How should the block result be improved?",
a:["Return a structured, non-retryable result naming the rule, explaining why, and stating the valid alternative action",
"Return nothing so the agent proceeds as though the call succeeded",
"Return a generic retryable error so the agent tries a different approach",
"Return the raw policy document so the agent can work out what applies"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Naming the rule, marking it non-retryable, and pointing at the alternative gives the agent everything it needs to respond well.",
"Silent success is the most dangerous option &mdash; the agent tells the customer the refund is processed when it is not.",
"Marking a deterministic policy block as retryable invites a retry storm that can never succeed.",
"Dumping a policy document into the context is unfocused, costly, and still does not say what to do next."],
e:"Hook blocks obey the same design rules as MCP tool errors, because from the model's perspective they are the same thing: a result it must act on. Carry an error category (business rule, not transient), an explicit retryability flag, a human-readable explanation, and the redirect. The two failure modes this avoids are the retry storm (ambiguous or seemingly-transient error) and the silent lie (block reported as success).",
k:"Block results need: rule name, reason, <code>isRetryable: false</code>, and the alternative action. Same contract as a well-designed tool error."},

{i:100,d:1,t:"1.5",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"Your agent's hooks have grown to fourteen, several overlapping, and nobody can predict what will happen to a given tool call.",
ask:"What is the most appropriate remediation?",
a:["Consolidate into a small number of hooks with clearly separated responsibilities and a documented execution order",
"Remove all hooks and move the rules into the system prompt",
"Keep the hooks but disable them in production to restore predictability",
"Add a fifteenth hook that logs what the other fourteen did"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The problem is unmanaged composition, so the fix is clear separation of concerns plus a defined, documented order.",
"Trades unpredictable enforcement for no enforcement, which is strictly worse for the rules that mattered.",
"Removes the controls from the only environment where they matter.",
"Improves observability without reducing the complexity that causes the unpredictability."],
e:"Hooks compose, and composition is where the surprises live: two hooks modifying the same argument, one blocking what another depends on, order-dependent behaviour nobody wrote down. Treat them as middleware &mdash; give each a single responsibility, define and document the order, make behaviour independent of order where you can, and test the composition rather than only the individual hooks. Consolidation usually reveals that several hooks were partial implementations of one rule.",
k:"Hooks are middleware. One responsibility each, documented order, tested as a composed pipeline &mdash; not as isolated units."},

{i:101,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"CCI",diff:2,
q:"You must review a pull request touching 25 files. A single prompt containing all 25 diffs produces shallow feedback that misses defects reviewers later find.",
ask:"Which decomposition strategy is appropriate?",
a:["Analyse each file in its own pass, then run a separate integration pass over the cross-file interactions",
"Send all 25 diffs again with an instruction to be more thorough",
"Review only the five largest files, since they carry most of the risk",
"Split the pull request into 25 separate pull requests"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Per-file passes give each file full attention; the integration pass catches what per-file review structurally cannot see.",
"Attention dilution is not fixed by asking for more effort; the same volume produces the same shallowness.",
"Arbitrary and unsafe &mdash; a one-line change to an authorisation check is higher risk than a 400-line generated file.",
"Restructures the team's workflow to work around a tool limitation, and destroys the reviewability of a coherent change."],
e:"Multi-pass review is named explicitly in the blueprint. Attention dilutes across a large input, so per-file passes recover local defect detection. But per-file review is blind to a whole class of problems &mdash; a signature changed in one file and not updated in its callers, a contract broken across a module boundary, a migration that does not match the model &mdash; which is why the separate cross-file integration pass is not optional. Combine with independent-instance review so the reviewer is not the generator.",
k:"Large reviews: per-file passes for local defects + a separate cross-file integration pass. One big prompt dilutes attention and misses both."},

{i:102,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"DPT",diff:3,
q:"An engineer asks the agent to investigate an intermittent production bug with no clear reproduction steps and no stack trace.",
ask:"Which decomposition approach fits?",
a:["Dynamic adaptive decomposition &mdash; map the relevant surface first, then generate the next investigative steps from what each finding reveals",
"A fixed pipeline: read the logs, then read the code, then write a report",
"Prompt chaining with a predetermined sequence of five analysis steps",
"Ask the engineer to supply reproduction steps before starting"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Open-ended investigation is the canonical case for adaptive decomposition, because the useful next step is unknowable in advance.",
"A fixed pipeline assumes you already know where the answer lives. For an intermittent bug with no trace, you do not.",
"Same objection: the sequence is predetermined, so it cannot follow the evidence where it actually leads.",
"Sometimes reasonable, but the premise is that reproduction steps do not exist &mdash; which is precisely why investigation is needed."],
e:"The blueprint contrasts two strategies. <strong>Fixed pipelines / prompt chaining</strong> suit predictable multi-aspect work where the steps are known up front &mdash; review each file, then integrate. <strong>Dynamic adaptive decomposition</strong> suits open-ended investigation where each finding determines the next subtask. The expected shape for the adaptive case is: map the structure first, build a prioritised hypothesis list, then investigate in priority order, revising the plan as evidence arrives.",
k:"Steps knowable up front &rarr; fixed pipeline. Next step depends on what you find &rarr; adaptive decomposition: map, prioritise, investigate, revise."},

{i:103,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"SDE",diff:2,
q:"You process 10,000 invoices nightly with a stable, well-understood extraction task: parse each document, validate the fields, write to the warehouse.",
ask:"Which decomposition strategy fits best?",
a:["A fixed pipeline, because the steps are identical and known in advance for every document",
"Dynamic adaptive decomposition, so the agent can choose a different approach per document",
"A coordinator with four specialised subagents per document",
"A single prompt containing all 10,000 documents"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Predictable, repeated, well-understood work is exactly where a fixed pipeline's determinism and auditability pay off.",
"Adaptivity buys nothing when every document takes the same path, and costs tokens, latency and predictability.",
"Multi-agent orchestration per document is heavy over-engineering for a three-step deterministic flow.",
"Far beyond any context window, and it destroys per-document error isolation and retry."],
e:"The exam tests this in both directions, so resist the pull toward the more sophisticated architecture. Sophistication earns its cost only when the domain has branching that a fixed design cannot express. Here the task is uniform, so a fixed pipeline gives determinism, straightforward per-document retry, clean auditability and the lowest cost &mdash; and it pairs naturally with the Message Batches API for a non-latency-sensitive overnight run.",
k:"Uniform, predictable, repeated work &rarr; fixed pipeline. Reserve agentic adaptivity for genuine branching. Do not over-architect."},

{i:104,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"MAR",diff:3,
q:"A coordinator decomposes &ldquo;evaluate our competitive position&rdquo; into three subtasks and dispatches them. The resulting report is coherent but misses the largest competitor entirely.",
ask:"Which change most reliably prevents this class of failure?",
a:["Require the coordinator to enumerate the full space explicitly before subdividing, and add a coverage check that names what was not examined",
"Increase the number of subtasks from three to six",
"Instruct each subagent to search more broadly within its subtask",
"Have the synthesis agent rewrite the report to sound less confident"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Enumerate first so the partition is visible and checkable, then verify coverage so gaps surface rather than hiding.",
"More slices of a badly drawn partition still miss whatever the partition excluded.",
"Subagents that faithfully cover their assigned scope cannot find what was never assigned to them.",
"Cosmetic. Hedged prose about a missing competitor is still a report that missed the competitor."],
e:"Coverage failures are assignment failures. The two-part defence is to make decomposition an explicit, inspectable step &mdash; the coordinator lists the dimensions of the space, states the partition and its exclusions, and only then dispatches &mdash; and to close the loop with a coverage check at synthesis that names what was not examined and why. This is the same diagnostic as the visual-art research failure: every component succeeded, so look at what was asked, not at who answered.",
k:"Enumerate the space before subdividing, state exclusions, and verify coverage at synthesis. Gaps are decomposition bugs, not worker bugs."},

{i:105,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"CGC",diff:2,
q:"A task requires generating an API client from an OpenAPI spec, then writing tests against it, then updating documentation. Each step depends on the previous step's output.",
ask:"Which approach fits?",
a:["Prompt chaining &mdash; a sequential pipeline where each step consumes the previous step's output",
"Three parallel subagents, one per step",
"A single prompt asking for all three artefacts at once",
"Adaptive decomposition, letting the agent decide the order each run"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Strict data dependencies define a chain, and chaining lets each step receive a validated input.",
"Parallelism requires independence. Tests cannot be written against a client that does not exist yet.",
"One prompt covering three substantial artefacts dilutes attention and produces mediocre versions of all three.",
"There is nothing to adapt: the order is fully determined by the dependencies."],
e:"Prompt chaining is the right structure whenever output N is input N+1. The benefits are focus (each step gets a dedicated prompt and full attention), verifiability (you can validate the generated client before writing tests against it), and cheap recovery (a bad step is re-run without redoing the others). Contrast it with parallel fan-out, which requires independence, and with adaptive decomposition, which requires genuine uncertainty about what comes next.",
k:"Data dependency &rarr; prompt chaining, with validation between steps. Independence &rarr; parallel fan-out. Uncertainty &rarr; adaptive."},

{i:106,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"DPT",diff:3,
q:"An agent is asked to &ldquo;improve performance of the checkout flow&rdquo; in an unfamiliar service. It immediately starts editing a database query it happened to notice.",
ask:"What should it do first?",
a:["Map the checkout flow's structure and identify where time is actually spent, then build a prioritised plan before changing anything",
"Edit the query, then measure whether it helped",
"Add caching to every database call in the flow",
"Ask the user which specific optimisation they want"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Map structure first, then prioritise by evidence &mdash; the prescribed opening move for an open-ended task.",
"Optimising the first thing you notice is guesswork; the query may be 2% of the latency.",
"Speculative caching across a flow introduces correctness risk (stale data) for unmeasured benefit.",
"The user asked for the outcome, not for a specific change &mdash; deciding what to optimise is the job."],
e:"For open-ended tasks the expected sequence is: map the structure, then build a prioritised plan, then execute against it. Jumping to an edit skips the step that determines whether the edit matters. This is also the natural boundary for planning mode in Claude Code &mdash; a task with multiple viable approaches and architectural consequence gets explored and planned before execution, whereas a bug with a clear stack trace in one file goes straight to direct execution.",
k:"Open-ended task: map structure &rarr; prioritise by evidence &rarr; execute. Never optimise the first thing you happen to notice."},

{i:107,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"CCI",diff:2,
q:"Your review agent produces excellent per-file feedback but repeatedly misses a defect where a function's signature changed in one file while three callers in other files were not updated.",
ask:"What is missing from the review architecture?",
a:["A cross-file integration pass that examines interactions between the changed files",
"A larger context window for the per-file passes",
"More detailed per-file review criteria",
"A second per-file pass over the same files"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. This defect exists only in the relationships between files, so no per-file pass can see it.",
"Window size is not the constraint &mdash; the reviewer was never shown the callers.",
"No amount of per-file criteria surfaces a caller that is not in the input.",
"Repeating a pass that structurally cannot see the problem yields the same blind spot twice."],
e:"Per-file and cross-file review find different defect classes, and you need both. Per-file passes catch local logic errors, missing validation, unsafe handling. The integration pass catches signature and contract mismatches, broken dataflow across modules, migrations inconsistent with models, and interface drift. Give the integration pass the right input &mdash; the set of changed symbols plus their call sites &mdash; rather than simply concatenating all the diffs, which reproduces the attention dilution you were avoiding.",
k:"Per-file passes catch local defects; a cross-file pass catches contract and dataflow breaks. Neither substitutes for the other."},

{i:108,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"A coordinator splits a research task into subtasks so fine-grained that it dispatches 40 subagents, each researching a single narrow question.",
ask:"What is the likely consequence?",
a:["High coordination overhead and fragmented findings that are hard to synthesise, with heavy duplication across the narrow slices",
"Improved coverage with no meaningful downside",
"The coordinator will exceed the maximum number of subagents allowed",
"Each subagent will produce identical output"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Over-decomposition costs orchestration overhead and produces fragments that are expensive to reassemble coherently.",
"Ignores the real cost curve: token spend, latency, aggregation complexity and duplicated context all rise.",
"The framing implies a hard platform limit that is not the substantive issue.",
"They will overlap in sources and framing, but not produce identical output &mdash; the problem is fragmentation, not duplication alone."],
e:"Decomposition has an optimum, not a direction. Too coarse and you get the shallow-coverage failure; too fine and you get overhead, duplication and a synthesis problem harder than the original task. The useful heuristic is to slice at natural seams &mdash; sub-domains a subject expert would recognise as distinct &mdash; and to size each slice so it needs a handful of tool calls rather than one. The blueprint explicitly names overly narrow decomposition as a coordinator risk.",
k:"Decomposition has an optimum. Too coarse &rarr; shallow coverage. Too fine &rarr; overhead, duplication, fragmented synthesis. Slice at natural seams."},

{i:109,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"CGC",diff:3,
q:"You are planning a migration touching roughly 45 files across three services, with several viable architectural approaches.",
ask:"How should the work be structured?",
a:["Use planning mode to explore and choose an approach, then decompose the chosen approach into ordered, verifiable execution phases",
"Start executing on the most obvious approach and adjust as problems appear",
"Ask the agent to make all 45 file changes in a single pass",
"Split into 45 independent tasks, one per file"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Architectural consequence plus multiple viable approaches is the textbook planning-mode trigger; phased execution follows the plan.",
"Reactive execution on a 45-file migration risks expensive rework once a structural problem surfaces halfway through.",
"No verification checkpoints, an unreviewable diff, and a context window that will not hold it.",
"The files are not independent &mdash; a migration has ordering constraints and shared contracts that per-file isolation destroys."],
e:"Two decisions combine. First, the planning-versus-direct-execution call: planning mode is for large changes, multiple viable approaches and architectural decisions, while direct execution is for simple, well-understood changes. Second, once the approach is chosen, decompose into ordered phases with verification between them &mdash; so a mistake surfaces at phase two rather than at file 45. Writing the plan to a file matters too: it survives compaction and lets each phase start from the plan rather than from the full history of the previous one.",
k:"Multiple viable approaches + architectural consequence &rarr; plan first, then execute in ordered, verified phases. Write the plan to a file."},

{i:110,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"SDE",diff:2,
q:"An extraction task must handle three document layouts that require genuinely different parsing strategies. Currently one prompt tries to handle all three.",
ask:"Which decomposition is appropriate?",
a:["Classify the layout first, then route to a strategy specialised for that layout",
"Add all three sets of instructions to the single prompt and let the model choose",
"Process every document with all three strategies and merge the results",
"Use adaptive decomposition so the agent invents a strategy per document"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Classify-then-route keeps each specialised prompt focused and makes per-layout accuracy measurable.",
"This is close to what is failing now: three instruction sets compete for attention and the model blends them.",
"Triples the cost and creates a merge problem, with no principled way to reconcile three disagreeing extractions.",
"There are exactly three known layouts, so inventing a strategy each time is unnecessary and unmeasurable."],
e:"When inputs fall into a small number of known classes with genuinely different handling, classify then route. Each specialised prompt stays focused, few-shot examples stay relevant to the layout at hand, and you gain per-layout accuracy metrics &mdash; which matters because aggregate accuracy famously masks poor performance on specific document types. Add an explicit <code>unknown</code> class that routes to human review rather than forcing an unfamiliar layout into the closest-looking strategy.",
k:"Small set of known input classes &rarr; classify then route to specialised handlers. Include an <code>unknown</code> class routed to human review."},

{i:111,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"CCI",diff:3,
q:"Your fixed three-stage review pipeline works well, but occasionally stage one finds something that makes stages two and three irrelevant &mdash; for example, the PR is a pure revert.",
ask:"What is the appropriate adjustment?",
a:["Add an early-exit condition after stage one so the pipeline can short-circuit when later stages are provably unnecessary",
"Convert the whole pipeline to fully adaptive decomposition",
"Remove stage one so the pipeline always runs stages two and three",
"Run all three stages in parallel so the wasted work costs no extra time"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A targeted early exit keeps the pipeline's determinism while eliminating the specific known waste.",
"A wholesale redesign to fix a narrow, well-understood case &mdash; sacrificing predictability and auditability for it.",
"Stage one is providing the useful signal; deleting it removes the detection.",
"Parallelism hides the latency but still burns the tokens, and stages two and three may depend on stage one's output."],
e:"Fixed pipelines are not required to be branch-free. Adding a small number of well-defined early exits or conditional stages preserves the determinism and auditability that motivated the pipeline while eliminating known waste. Reach for full adaptive decomposition only when the branching is genuinely open-ended &mdash; when you cannot enumerate the conditions in advance. A handful of named conditions is a pipeline with guards, not an agentic investigation.",
k:"Fixed pipelines can carry named conditional exits. Go fully adaptive only when the branch conditions cannot be enumerated up front."},

{i:112,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"You are choosing between decomposing a task into sub-prompts within one agent, versus into separate subagents.",
ask:"Which consideration most strongly favours separate subagents?",
a:["The subtasks produce large volumes of intermediate output that would crowd the shared context",
"The subtasks are described in different sections of the requirements document",
"There are more than three subtasks",
"The subtasks were written by different team members"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Context isolation &mdash; keeping verbose intermediate work out of the parent window &mdash; is a primary justification for delegation.",
"How the requirements are organised says nothing about the runtime characteristics that matter.",
"Count alone is not a criterion; ten small dependent steps belong in one agent.",
"Authorship of the requirements is irrelevant to the architecture."],
e:"Two criteria justify separate subagents: independence (enabling parallelism) and verbose intermediate output (enabling context isolation). Everything else &mdash; how many there are, how the spec is structured, who wrote it &mdash; is noise. When neither criterion holds, sub-prompts within one agent are simpler, cheaper and often produce better results, because continuous context lets later steps build naturally on what earlier ones established.",
k:"Subagents when subtasks are independent <em>or</em> produce verbose intermediate output. Otherwise sequential sub-prompts in one agent."},

{i:113,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"After a first round of research, the coordinator sees that one subtopic returned thin results while another surfaced an unexpected and important angle.",
ask:"What should an adaptive coordinator do?",
a:["Generate a second round of subtasks informed by the first round &mdash; re-scoping the thin subtopic and dispatching an agent to the new angle",
"Proceed to synthesis with what it has, since the planned subtasks are complete",
"Re-run the identical subtasks in the hope of better results",
"Discard the unexpected angle because it was not in the original plan"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Revising the plan from intermediate results is precisely what adaptive decomposition means.",
"Treats a plan as a contract rather than a hypothesis, and ships a report with a known thin section and a known missing angle.",
"Identical inputs produce substantially identical outputs; nothing has changed.",
"Discarding an important finding because it was unplanned is the most costly option &mdash; that discovery is the value of doing research."],
e:"Adaptive decomposition means the plan is revised as evidence arrives. Two triggers appear here: a subtask that underdelivered, needing re-scoping or a different source strategy; and a discovery that opens a dimension the original partition missed. Both call for a second dispatch round. This is also the iterative-refinement loop in action &mdash; the coordinator evaluates intermediate results against its quality criteria and re-routes, rather than passing whatever arrives straight to synthesis.",
k:"Adaptive coordinators revise the plan from intermediate results: re-scope thin subtasks, dispatch to newly discovered angles, then synthesise."},

{i:114,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"CGC",diff:2,
q:"A bug report includes a clear stack trace pointing to a single function in one file, with an obvious off-by-one error.",
ask:"Which approach is appropriate?",
a:["Direct execution &mdash; fix the specific defect and add a regression test",
"Planning mode, to explore the codebase before making any change",
"A multi-agent investigation with three parallel hypotheses",
"A full architectural review of the module containing the bug"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A simple, well-understood change with a clear diagnosis is the textbook direct-execution case.",
"Planning mode is for large changes with multiple viable approaches. Here there is one obvious fix.",
"Elaborate machinery for a defect that has already been located precisely.",
"Unrequested scope expansion that delays a straightforward fix."],
e:"The exam tests the planning-versus-direct-execution decision from both directions, and over-planning is as wrong as under-planning. Direct execution suits simple, well-understood changes: a clear stack trace, a single file, an obvious defect. Planning mode suits large changes, multiple viable approaches and architectural decisions. The regression test is part of the correct answer &mdash; it converts &ldquo;fix the bug&rdquo; into a verifiable goal.",
k:"Clear diagnosis + single file + obvious fix &rarr; direct execution with a regression test. Do not over-plan trivial changes."},

{i:115,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"SDE",diff:3,
q:"Your pipeline extracts data, validates it, and on failure retries with feedback. You want to add a step that catches semantic errors such as line items that do not sum to the stated total.",
ask:"Where does this step belong and how should it work?",
a:["In validation, by extracting both the computed total and the stated total so code can compare them and feed any discrepancy into the retry",
"In the extraction prompt, by instructing the model to check its arithmetic",
"After persistence, as a nightly reconciliation job",
"In the JSON Schema, by adding a constraint that the totals must match"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Extract both values so the comparison becomes a deterministic check, and turn the discrepancy into specific retry feedback.",
"Self-checked arithmetic in the same pass is unreliable, and it produces no artefact your pipeline can verify.",
"Detects the problem after bad data is already in the warehouse and downstream consumers have read it.",
"JSON Schema validates structure and types; it cannot express a cross-field arithmetic relationship."],
e:"The blueprint's self-correction pattern is precisely this: extract <code>calculated_total</code> alongside <code>stated_total</code> so a discrepancy becomes visible to code rather than depending on the model's arithmetic. It also draws the syntax/semantics line clearly &mdash; <code>tool_use</code> with a JSON Schema eliminates <em>syntax</em> errors (malformed JSON, missing fields, wrong types) but does nothing about <em>semantic</em> errors (totals that do not reconcile, values in the wrong field). Semantics need domain validation in code.",
k:"Schemas kill syntax errors, not semantic ones. Extract both computed and stated values so code can reconcile them, then retry with the specific discrepancy."},

{i:116,d:1,t:"1.7",s:"CGC",diff:2,
q:"You want to continue yesterday's named investigation session exactly where it left off, and nothing on disk has changed since.",
ask:"Which mechanism applies?",
a:["<code>--resume</code> with the session name",
"<code>fork_session</code> from the session",
"A new session seeded with a summary",
"<code>/compact</code> followed by a fresh prompt"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Resuming a named session is exactly this case: continue where you left off with context still valid.",
"Forking creates a branch, which is for exploring alternatives, not for straightforward continuation.",
"Appropriate when context has gone stale. Nothing has changed here, so you would be discarding valid context.",
"Compaction reduces an <em>active</em> session's context; it is not a mechanism for resuming a previous one."],
e:"Keep the three mechanisms distinct, because the exam presents all three as options in every session question. <code>--resume &lt;name&gt;</code>: continue a named session, correct when the accumulated context is still accurate. <code>fork_session</code>: branch from shared context into independent lines, correct for comparing alternatives. Fresh session plus a structured summary: correct when the context has gone stale, most commonly because files changed on disk.",
k:"Context valid &rarr; <code>--resume</code>. Comparing alternatives &rarr; <code>fork_session</code>. Context stale &rarr; fresh session + structured summary."},

{i:117,d:1,t:"1.7",s:"CGC",diff:3,
q:"A colleague argues that resuming a session is always preferable to starting fresh, because it preserves context and saves time.",
ask:"What is the correct nuance?",
a:["Resuming preserves stale context as readily as valid context; when the underlying files or facts have changed, a fresh session with an accurate summary is more reliable",
"Resuming is always preferable, as the colleague says",
"Starting fresh is always preferable, because stale context is a constant risk",
"Resuming is only safe within the same calendar day"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Resume preserves everything, including what has since become false &mdash; validity is the deciding factor.",
"Ignores the staleness failure mode the blueprint explicitly warns about.",
"Over-corrects and discards genuinely valid context, paying the full re-exploration cost every time.",
"Time elapsed is a weak proxy. A session is stale the moment the underlying files change, whether that is in ten minutes or ten days."],
e:"The blueprint states that a new session with a structured summary can be <em>more</em> reliable than resuming with stale results. Session context is a cache of the world as it was when you read it. The decision rule is about validity, not recency: if nothing relevant has changed, resume; if files moved, were renamed, or were refactored, either explicitly tell the session what changed or start fresh with a summary of goal, decisions made and current state.",
k:"Resume preserves stale context just as faithfully as valid context. Decide on validity, not on elapsed time."},

{i:118,d:1,t:"1.7",s:"CGC",diff:2,
q:"You are about to compact a long session. You want to make sure the specific version numbers, file paths and measured timings you discovered are not lost.",
ask:"What should you do first?",
a:["Write those specifics to a scratchpad file before compacting, so they survive independently of the conversation",
"Repeat them in your next message so they appear recently in the context",
"Trust compaction to preserve important details automatically",
"Raise the context window so compaction is not needed"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A file survives compaction entirely and can be re-read on demand, which is why scratchpads are the named remedy.",
"Recency helps briefly and is itself subject to the next compaction &mdash; a delay, not a fix.",
"Progressive summarisation is exactly where numeric values, percentages and dates get condensed into vague statements.",
"Not always available, and it postpones rather than solves &mdash; the same session running longer hits the same wall."],
e:"Progressive summarisation is lossy in a specific and predictable way: precise values are the first casualty, replaced by qualitative paraphrase (&ldquo;performance improved significantly&rdquo; in place of &ldquo;p95 fell from 840ms to 310ms&rdquo;). Externalising to a scratchpad file &mdash; findings, decisions, exact paths, measured numbers &mdash; makes them immune, re-readable, and survivable across a crash or a fresh session. Write it as you go, not when you notice the context filling.",
k:"Compaction destroys numbers, dates and paths first. Externalise specifics to a scratchpad file <em>before</em> compacting."},

{i:119,d:1,t:"1.7",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"A long-running agent crashes mid-workflow after completing 7 of 12 phases. On restart it begins again from phase 1.",
ask:"What is missing from the design?",
a:["Structured state persistence recording which phases completed and their outputs, so a restart resumes from phase 8",
"A longer timeout so the agent does not crash",
"A larger context window so the workflow fits in one run",
"An instruction telling the agent to remember what it has already done"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Durable checkpointing after each phase is what makes recovery possible; in-memory context does not survive a crash.",
"Reduces crash frequency without changing what happens when one occurs.",
"Same objection &mdash; it makes the run more likely to finish, and provides no recovery when it does not.",
"There is no context to remember from; the process died. Memory instructions cannot survive process death."],
e:"Structured state persistence for crash recovery is named explicitly in the blueprint. Checkpoint after each phase: which phases completed, their outputs or the artefact paths, and what remains. On restart, read the state and resume from the first incomplete phase. This pairs naturally with idempotent operations &mdash; if a phase was interrupted midway, re-running it must be safe &mdash; and with scratchpad files, which serve as both the checkpoint and the context carrier for the resumed run.",
k:"Checkpoint phase completion and outputs to durable state. Combine with idempotent phases so an interrupted phase can safely re-run."},

{i:120,d:1,t:"1.7",s:"CGC",diff:2,
q:"You forked a session to try two refactoring approaches. Approach B clearly wins. You now want to continue the real work.",
ask:"What is the cleanest way to proceed?",
a:["Continue in branch B and abandon branch A, carrying forward a summary of why B was chosen",
"Merge both branches back into the original session",
"Return to the original pre-fork session and redo approach B there",
"Keep both branches active in case A becomes relevant later"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Branch B already holds the full working context for the chosen approach; recording the rationale preserves the decision.",
"Sessions are conversational contexts, not version-controlled branches &mdash; there is no merge operation, and merging two divergent reasoning traces would be incoherent.",
"Discards B's accumulated work and repeats it, which is exactly the cost forking existed to avoid.",
"Maintaining a dead branch costs attention and invites confusion about which context is current."],
e:"Forking is a decision-making device: branch, explore in parallel, pick a winner, continue in it. There is no merge &mdash; a session is a linear conversation, not a DAG. The one thing worth carrying forward explicitly is the rationale (&ldquo;chose B because it avoids the circular dependency between the payments and ledger modules&rdquo;), because that reasoning lived in a comparison the surviving branch never saw in full. Write it into the branch or into a scratchpad file.",
k:"Fork &rarr; explore &rarr; pick a winner &rarr; continue in it. Sessions do not merge. Carry the decision rationale forward explicitly."},

{i:121,d:1,t:"1.7",s:"CCI",diff:3,
q:"Your CI runs Claude Code on every push. A developer notices that the second run on a branch appears to remember the first run's findings, and reports it as a bug.",
ask:"What is the correct expectation and design?",
a:["CI runs should be isolated by default; if prior findings are needed, pass them explicitly as input rather than relying on implicit session carryover",
"CI runs should always share one long-lived session so context accumulates",
"CI runs should never receive any prior context under any circumstances",
"The developer is mistaken; CI sessions cannot retain context"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Isolation by default, with explicit and auditable context passing when continuity is genuinely wanted.",
"A shared long-lived session accumulates unbounded irrelevant context and lets one branch's findings contaminate another's review.",
"Too absolute &mdash; passing previous review results is explicitly useful so a re-run reports only new or unfixed issues.",
"Session state can be retained if configured that way; the question is whether it should be."],
e:"Session isolation matters twice in CI. First, correctness: a review instance that shares context with the generation run is a worse reviewer, so review should be independent. Second, reproducibility: implicit carryover makes runs non-deterministic and hard to debug. The blueprint does recommend including prior review results when re-running after new commits &mdash; so the bot reports only new or unfixed issues rather than repeating itself &mdash; but that context should be passed explicitly as input, not inherited invisibly.",
k:"CI sessions: isolated by default. Pass prior review results explicitly as input so re-runs report only new or unfixed issues."},

{i:122,d:1,t:"1.7",s:"DPT",diff:2,
q:"An investigation session has grown to fill most of its context window. The task is only half done, and you need the remaining half to be accurate.",
ask:"Which sequence is best?",
a:["Write current findings and remaining questions to a scratchpad, then start a fresh session seeded with that file",
"Run <code>/compact</code> and keep going without recording anything",
"Keep going and accept that quality will degrade",
"Delete the earliest messages manually and continue"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Externalise first so nothing is lost, then start clean so the remaining work runs with full headroom and accurate context.",
"Compaction without externalising first is where the specific values and paths get summarised into vagueness.",
"Accepts the known degradation pattern &mdash; vague, pattern-level answers &mdash; on work that must be accurate.",
"Position-based deletion removes findings by age rather than by relevance, which is the sliding-window mistake."],
e:"The safe order is <strong>externalise, then reset</strong>. Write findings, decisions, exact file paths, measured values and open questions to a scratchpad; then start fresh seeded with that file. You get full context headroom, an accurate rather than summarised carryover, and an artefact that survives crashes and can be handed to a colleague. <code>/compact</code> is the lighter-weight option when you simply need breathing room mid-task, but it is lossy and should follow, never replace, externalising the specifics.",
k:"Externalise findings to a file, <em>then</em> compact or restart. Never compact first and hope the specifics survive."},

{i:123,d:1,t:"1.7",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"You need two agents to work on the same problem with completely independent perspectives, with no risk of one influencing the other.",
ask:"Which approach guarantees independence?",
a:["Spawn two separate subagents with their own isolated contexts and identical inputs",
"Fork one session into two branches",
"Ask one agent to produce two independent analyses",
"Run one agent twice in the same session and compare the outputs"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Separate contexts starting from the same inputs is the only configuration with no shared reasoning to contaminate them.",
"Forked branches share everything that accumulated <em>before</em> the fork, so any bias formed pre-fork is inherited by both.",
"A single context means the second analysis is anchored on the first &mdash; the opposite of independence.",
"The second run sees the first run's output in the shared context, so it anchors on it."],
e:"Independence and shared context are opposites, and each session mechanism sits at a different point on that trade-off. Fully independent subagents cost the most (each rebuilds its context from the inputs) and give the strongest guarantee &mdash; the right choice for adversarial review, second opinions and any measurement where correlated errors would be misleading. Forking is the middle ground: cheaper, because expensive setup context is shared, but only independent from the fork point onward.",
k:"Independence &rarr; separate subagents, same inputs, no shared context. Forks share everything before the fork point."},

{i:124,d:1,t:"1.7",s:"CGC",diff:3,
q:"You want a resumed session to know that three specific files were modified externally since it last ran.",
ask:"What is the most reliable way to handle this?",
a:["State explicitly which files changed and have the session re-read them, replacing the stale content in its context",
"Assume the session will re-read files automatically when it next needs them",
"Run <code>/compact</code> so the stale content is summarised away",
"Increase the context window so both old and new versions fit"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Explicit invalidation plus a fresh read is what actually replaces the stale content.",
"There is no automatic re-read. The session works from what is already in its context.",
"Compaction may summarise the stale content, but a summary of stale content is still stale &mdash; and now less specific.",
"Holding both versions is worse: the model must pick between contradictory copies of the same file with no signal about which is current."],
e:"Treat session context as a cache with no invalidation protocol &mdash; you have to invalidate it yourself. Naming the changed files and asking for a re-read makes the fresh content the most recent thing in context, which also helps salience. If many files changed, or if the changes are structural (renames, moves, deletions), the cheaper and safer move is a fresh session seeded with a structured summary, because piecemeal invalidation of a heavily-changed world is error-prone.",
k:"Session context is an uninvalidated cache. Name changed files and re-read them; if the change is large or structural, start fresh instead."},

{i:125,d:1,t:"1.7",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"An agent's session state grows to include every tool result from a 200-step workflow. Later steps become slow and the agent starts missing details from the middle of the run.",
ask:"Which two measures address this most directly?",
a:["Trim tool results to relevant fields as they arrive, and checkpoint completed phases to external state rather than carrying them in context",
"Increase the context window and reduce the number of steps",
"Lower the temperature and shorten the system prompt",
"Summarise the whole session after every step"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Attack the inflow (trim results) and the accumulation (externalise completed phases) &mdash; the two mechanisms actually causing the growth.",
"Scaling and scope reduction avoid the problem rather than managing it, and a 200-step workflow will outgrow any window.",
"Neither temperature nor system-prompt length has any bearing on accumulated tool output.",
"Summarising after every step is expensive, compounds loss with each pass, and destroys precisely the specifics from the middle of the run."],
e:"Two failure modes are described and they have different causes. Slowness comes from raw context volume, addressed by trimming inflow and externalising what is finished. Missing details from the middle is the lost-in-the-middle effect, addressed by keeping the live context small and by placing key findings at the beginning of aggregated data with explicit headings. Together: trim what arrives, persist what is done, and position what remains deliberately.",
k:"Long workflows: trim tool results on arrival, checkpoint finished phases externally, and position key findings at the start of what remains."}
);
